(Bug #91, the slots a call owns. A `text`, `list` or `map` destination is
 not only a declaration: a function's declared return type is one, and so
 is a parameter. A miss handed either of those the raw 0 and the first read
 in the receiving frame dereferenced it. Inside a function the list is a
 parameter, so its elements carry runtime tags and no static element type
 can be proved - the corner no compile-time proof reaches at all.)

To peek with a list called shelf. Return a text, element 5 of shelf.
To 'peek in range' with a list called shelf. Return a text, element 2 of shelf.
To announce with a text called label. Print "announced: [{label}]".

a list called grown is ["a", "b"].
Append "c" to grown.

(The working neighbour: a return the list can satisfy.)
a text called found is 'peek in range' of grown.
Print "returned in range: {found}".

(The return slot: the miss travels back as the empty text.)
a text called missed is peek of grown.
Print "returned past the end: [{missed}]".

(The parameter slot: the miss travels in as the empty text.)
announce of element 5 of grown.

(A returned miss is a real text, so the caller can go on using it.)
a list called collected is [].
Append missed to collected.
Print "collected: {collected}".

(And the miss is still an error in the caller's frame.)
a text called 'last miss' is peek of grown.
On error Print "the error flag was set".
